<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Configuration for the external (V4L2/UVC) camera HAL.

  A PC has no built-in camera sensor; what it has is USB ports, and a webcam is
  a UVC device that shows up as /dev/video*. So the external camera provider is
  the honest HAL for this target rather than an emulated one, and it needs this
  file: ExternalCameraUtils.cpp reads /vendor/etc/external_camera_config.xml and
  falls back to built-in defaults if it is missing.

  The <ignore> list is for internal video nodes that are not cameras. It is
  empty here deliberately: on a PC every /dev/video* really is a capture device
  as far as this target is concerned. Note that a single webcam often exposes
  TWO nodes (one capture, one metadata); the HAL probes and skips the ones
  that cannot stream, so they do not need listing.

  The FpsList does NOT do what its name suggests, and getting this wrong broke
  video recording. getFrameRateList() (ExternalCameraDevice.cpp) enumerates the
  rates the camera itself reports and DISCARDS every one above fpsBound; it
  never invents a rate. If nothing survives, updateFpsBounds() then drops the
  whole resolution from the supported list. So a low bound does not gently cap
  a fast mode, it deletes it.

  That matters because Camera API1 (still used by the video path of the stock
  camera app) filters preview sizes to those reaching 29.97fps:
  Parameters.cpp:3071 drops any size whose minFrameDuration exceeds
  1e9 / 29.97, and aborts with "generated preview size list is empty!!" if none
  remain. minFrameDuration is derived from the FASTEST rate that survived the
  bound above. Bounds of 15 and 10 therefore made 720p and 1080p arithmetically
  incapable of ever clearing that floor, leaving API1 with nothing unless the
  camera happened to offer MJPEG at 640x480 or below. This one does not, so
  Camera2Client failed to initialise with BAD_VALUE and the app called finish().

  The bounds below sit above each nominal rate so the hardware's own rates pass
  through untouched. This cannot overstate what the camera can do: the values
  are a ceiling applied to the device's reported list, so anything advertised
  was measured by the camera, not by us. The old bounds were copied from
  dragonboard and yukawa, where they guard USB 2.0 bandwidth on an external
  webcam; on a built-in laptop sensor they only removed working modes.

  Each bound is the nominal rate plus one. UVC expresses frame intervals in
  100ns units, so 30fps is 333333 rather than 333333.33 and reads back as
  30.00003fps. A bound of exactly 30.0 would compare greater-than and discard
  the very mode it was meant to admit; the margin absorbs that rounding.

  1920x1080 stays the last entry, so anything larger is still unsupported: the
  list ceiling is what bounds resolution, and MaxJpegBufferSize above is sized
  for 1080p.
-->
<ExternalCamera>
    <Provider>
        <ignore> <!-- Internal video devices to be ignored by external camera HAL -->
        </ignore>
    </Provider>
    <!-- See ExternalCameraUtils.cpp for default values of Device configurations below -->
    <Device>
        <!-- Max JPEG buffer size in bytes-->
        <MaxJpegBufferSize bytes="3145728"/> <!-- 3MB (~= 1080p YUV420) -->
        <!-- Size of v4l2 buffer queue when streaming >= 30fps -->
        <!-- Larger value: more request can be cached pipeline (less janky)  -->
        <!-- Smaller value: use less memory -->
        <NumVideoBuffers count="4"/>
        <!-- Size of v4l2 buffer queue when streaming < 30fps -->
        <NumStillBuffers count="2"/>

        <!-- List of maximum fps for various output sizes -->
        <!-- Any image size smaller than the size listed in Limit row will report
            fps (as minimum frame duration) up to the fpsBound value. -->
        <FpsList>
            <!-- width/height must be increasing, fpsBound must be decreasing-->
            <Limit width="640" height="480" fpsBound="121.0"/>
            <Limit width="1280" height="720" fpsBound="61.0"/>
            <Limit width="1920" height="1080" fpsBound="31.0"/>
            <!-- image size larger than the last entry will not be supported-->
        </FpsList>
    </Device>
</ExternalCamera>
